issue_context.body:
**Describe the bug**
`getsockname()` fails, setting `errno` to `EINVAL` when there is no connection handler, even when a socket has been bound with `bind()`.

The exact snippet is
https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/038351ad2615531e0e1ddbc47d907f16bb62ea50/subsys/net/lib/sockets/sockets.c#L2390-L2393

So either the connection handler is not getting set within the call to `bind()` or this check is invalid.

Please also mention any information which could help others to understand
the problem you're facing:
 - What target platform are you using? `qemu_x86`
 - What have you tried to diagnose or workaround this issue? step-debugged, found broken check
 - Is this a regression? Unsure.

**To Reproduce**
Steps to reproduce the behavior:
1. see sample code below

**Expected behavior**
`getsockname()` should return successfully, returning the assigned sockaddr

**Impact**
This is very clearly broken, and will affect any service using an ephemeral port number that wishes to obtain the assigned port number from the network subsystem.

**Logs and console output**
```
*** Booting Zephyr OS build v3.4.0-rc2-150-g83587a3cbca9 ***
I: Type 'quit' to quit
E: getsockname: 22
```

**Environment (please complete the following information):**
 - OS: Linux
 - Toolchain (e.g Zephyr SDK, ...): Zephyr SDK v0.16.1
 - Commit SHA or Version used: e0e7f5888f63555fbfa028331dfaf450d004d965

**Additional context**
Discovered in the context of working on #59669

Sample code:
```cpp
		/* Create a socket */
		ret = socket(af, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
		if (ret < 0) {
			LOG_ERR("socket: %d", errno);
			ret = -errno;
			break;
		}
		fd = ret;

		if (setsockopt(fd, SOL_SOCKET, SO_REUSEADDR, &(int){1}, sizeof(int)) < 0) {
			LOG_ERR("setsockopt: %d", errno);
			ret = -errno;
			break;
		}

		/* Bind to the specified address */
		if (bind(fd, &addr4, len) < 0) {
			LOG_ERR("bind: %d", errno);
			ret = -errno;
			break;
		}

		if (*port == 0) {
			/* ephemeral port - read back the port number */
			len = sizeof(addr4);
			ret = getsockname(fd, &addr4, &len);
			if (ret < 0) {
				LOG_ERR("getsockname: %d", errno);
				ret = -errno;
				break;
			}
			*port = addr4.sin_port;
		}
```

comments:
Just to illustrate that the result of `getsockname()` should be in network byte order.

```shell
gcc -o /tmp/blah /tmp/blah.c
/tmp/blah &
# connect to the one that says "host byte order"
# should get an error like "curl: (7) Failed to connect to localhost port 56711 after 0 ms: Connection refused"
# connect to the one that says "network byte order"
# connect should be successful, but since we immediately close, this will print "curl: (52) Empty reply from server"
```

```cpp
#include <netinet/in.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>

int main()
{
  int fd;
  int fd2;
  socklen_t len;
  struct sockaddr_in addr = {0};

  fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
  if (fd < 0) {
    perror("socket");
  }
  if (setsockopt(fd, SOL_SOCKET, SO_REUSEADDR, &(int){1}, sizeof(int)) < 0) {
    perror("setsockopt");
  }
  len = sizeof(addr);
  if (bind(fd, (struct sockaddr *)&addr, len) < 0) {
    perror("bind");
  }
  len = sizeof(addr);
  if (getsockname(fd, (struct sockaddr *)&addr, &len) < 0) {
    perror("getsockname");
  }

  if (listen(fd, 10) < 0) {
    perror("listen");
  }

  printf("accepting connections at\n");
  printf("curl 'localhost:%u' # (host byte order), or\n", addr.sin_port);
  printf("curl 'localhost:%u' # (network byte order)\n", ntohs(addr.sin_port));

  len = sizeof(addr);
  fd2 = accept(fd, (struct sockaddr *)&addr, &len);
  if (fd2 < 0) {
    perror("accept");
  }

  close(fd);
  close(fd2);

  return 0;
}
```
